From: pas7std co-op <coop@pas7std.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 0003/0003] NPC capacitor: advance it without a session and give every hull a recharge rate

runtime.js advances a ship capacitor only inside active module cycle bookkeeping,
so a native NPC with no session never recovered: a strip miner activated once and
then failed with NOT_ENOUGH_CAPACITOR forever.

Two halves of the same defect:
  * the CCP non-linear curve (with the 1% floor) is advanced for sessionless
    ships from the behaviour loop;
  * the recharge rate is a property of the HULL, but it was only filled in by
    nativeNpcService, which co-op mining fleets never pass through. Those hulls
    kept tau = 0 and the new advance returned immediately (live evidence:
    fleet 2 miners at 3-5% charge, NOT_ENOUGH_CAPACITOR every cycle). The rate
    is now resolved lazily from the same dogma attribute the fitting service
    uses and cached on the entity.

Every sessionless ship in the scene is recharged once a second by the scene
tick, not only ships that happen to carry an NPC controller: co-op mining hulls
have none, and live telemetry showed tau set but capacitorRechargedAtMs = 0
forever, so their charge only ever fell.

No capacitor is granted out of thin air: the same curve real ships use.

The advance measured its own interval against capacitorRechargedAtMs, a shared
client-visible stamp that other code also refreshes. The interval therefore
collapsed to milliseconds per call: measured 0.00009/s of charge against the
0.0048/s the CCP curve implies, so a hull that fell to 2% never recovered and
every mining activation failed with NOT_ENOUGH_CAPACITOR. A private stamp
records the interval this advance actually covers.

